19:T10b0,## The idea: a snapshot you can put back

You want Ctrl-Z. You need to record what an object looked like *before* a change, hold onto it, and later put the object back exactly the way it was. The naive fix is to expose the object's guts so something else can read and rewind them, but now that "something else" depends on every private field, and your **encapsulation is gone**.

Memento solves this by letting the object snapshot *itself* into a **sealed token** that nobody else can open. You can stash the token anywhere, pass it around, build a stack of them, and only the original object knows how to read one back.

> **In one line:** capture internal state into an *opaque* snapshot you can stash and restore later, undo/redo without leaking how the object works inside.

## An analogy: save points in a game

Think of a video game **save point**. When you save, the game writes a snapshot of your entire world, health, inventory, position, quest flags, into a save slot. The save slot is opaque: you can't open it and hand-edit your health. But you can keep *many* of them, and when you die you **reload** the last one and the world snaps back. The save *file* is the Memento, the *game* is the Originator, and the *save-slot manager* is the Caretaker that lists your saves without understanding a single byte inside them. Ctrl-Z in a document is the same trick at smaller granularity.

## The three roles

| Role | Who | Job |
|---|---|---|
| **Originator** | `Editor` | Creates a memento of its state; restores itself from one. The *only* one that can read inside. |
| **Memento** | snapshot token | A frozen copy of the originator's state. Opaque to everyone but the originator. |
| **Caretaker** | `History` | Keeps the undo stack of mementos. Stashes and returns them, **never peeks inside**. |

```
class Editor {                                             // ── Originator
    String text;
    Memento save()         { return new Memento(text); }   // snapshot self
    void restore(Memento m){ this.text = m.getText(); }    // only Editor reads it
}
class History {                                            // ── Caretaker
    Stack<Memento> stack;                                  // opaque tokens
    void push(Memento m){ stack.push(m); }
    Memento pop()        { return stack.pop(); }           // never looks inside
}
```

## Why encapsulation survives

The clever part: the **caretaker holds state it cannot read**. `History` shuffles mementos onto and off its stack without ever touching the editor's internals. Because only the `Editor` can open a `Memento`, the editor is free to change its private representation, add a cursor position, switch `text` to a rope data structure, and **none of the undo machinery breaks**. The boundary holds: state crosses it, knowledge does not.

## Versus Command-based undo

There are two classic ways to implement undo, and interviewers love to contrast them:

| | **Memento** | **Command** |
|---|---|---|
| How undo works | **restore a whole snapshot** of the prior state | **replay the inverse** action (`undo()` calls `light.off()`) |
| Each operation must… | be *copyable* | be *invertible* |
| Cost | **memory**, a full copy per step | **CPU / care**, every command needs a correct reverse |
| Best when | state is small or hard to invert | actions are cleanly reversible (insert ↔ delete) |
| Redo | re-restore the next snapshot | re-run the original command |

Reversing is cheap on memory but every operation must be invertible; snapshots work for *anything* but cost a copy each. Real editors often **blend the two**, full snapshots at checkpoints, lightweight inverse deltas in between.

## The cost of snapshots

> **Watch the memory.** A naive memento is a *full deep copy* of the originator on every save, a 50-page document times 200 undo steps is a lot of RAM. Mitigations: store **incremental / diff mementos** (just what changed since the last snapshot), **cap the stack** to the last N states, snapshot only at **checkpoints**, or **share immutable substructure** (structural sharing / persistent data structures) so unchanged parts aren't copied at all.

This same pattern is the engine behind Undo/Redo, editor history, game save points, and database transaction rollbacks.c:["$","div",null,{"className":"flex min-h-screen","children":[["$","$L17",null,{"activeModule":"lld-behavioral","activeLesson":"memento"}],["$","$L18","memento",{"lesson":{"id":"memento","patternId":"lld-behavioral","title":"Memento","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"Core","statement":"Capture an object's internal state into an opaque snapshot you can stash away and restore later, implementing undo without breaking encapsulation.","companies":["Amazon","Adobe","Figma"],"theory":"$19","approaches":[{"label":"Snapshot & restore","code":["class Editor {","    String text;","    Memento save()        { return new Memento(text); }","    void restore(Memento m){ text = m.getText(); }","}","class History {","    Stack<Memento> stack;","    void push(Memento m){ stack.push(m); }","    Memento pop()        { return stack.pop(); }","}"],"steps":[{"view":"class","codeLine":3,"narration":"The Editor (Originator) can save() its state into a Memento and restore(m) from one. It alone knows how to read the snapshot it creates, to everyone else the Memento is opaque.","classDiagram":{"classes":[{"id":"orig","name":"Editor","fields":["- text"],"methods":["+ save(): Memento","+ restore(m)"],"x":0,"y":0.3,"tint":"accent"},{"id":"mem","name":"Memento","fields":["- text"],"methods":["(opaque)"],"x":1.6,"y":0.3},{"id":"care","name":"History","fields":["- stack"],"methods":["+ push(m)","+ pop()"],"x":1.6,"y":1.6,"tint":"accent2"}],"relations":[{"id":"creates","from":"orig","to":"mem","kind":"depend","label":"creates"},{"id":"keeps","from":"care","to":"mem","kind":"aggregate","label":"keeps","toMult":"*"}],"shownClassIds":["orig","mem"],"shownRelIds":["creates"],"activeIds":["orig","mem"]},"vars":[["Originator","snapshots itself"],["Memento","opaque token"]]},{"view":"class","codeLine":7,"narration":"The History (Caretaker) keeps a stack of Mementos, but treats them as opaque, never reading inside. It holds the editor's state without ever understanding it.","classDiagram":{"classes":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:0:steps:0:classDiagram:classes","relations":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:0:steps:0:classDiagram:relations","shownClassIds":["orig","mem","care"],"shownRelIds":["creates","keeps"],"activeIds":["care"]},"vars":[["Caretaker","holds, never opens"]]},{"view":"sequence","codeLine":3,"narration":"Run #1. The User saves with text \"A\": the Editor snapshots itself into memento mA and pushes it onto History. The stack now holds one snapshot.","sequence":{"actors":[{"id":"u","label":"User","kind":"actor"},{"id":"e","label":"Editor","tint":"accent"},{"id":"h","label":"History","tint":"accent2"}],"messages":[{"id":"m1","from":"u","to":"e","label":"save(): \"A\"","kind":"call"},{"id":"m2","from":"e","to":"h","label":"push(mA)","kind":"call"},{"id":"m3","from":"u","to":"e","label":"save(): \"AB\"","kind":"call"},{"id":"m4","from":"e","to":"h","label":"push(mAB)","kind":"call"},{"id":"m5","from":"u","to":"e","label":"type \"C\" → \"ABC\"","kind":"call"},{"id":"m6","from":"u","to":"e","label":"undo()","kind":"call"},{"id":"m7","from":"e","to":"h","label":"pop()","kind":"call"},{"id":"m8","from":"h","to":"e","label":"mAB","kind":"return"}],"shownCount":2,"activeIndex":1},"vars":[["text","\"A\""],["stack ↑","[ mA ]"]]},{"view":"sequence","codeLine":3,"narration":"Save #2. The User edits to \"AB\" and saves again: a second memento mAB is pushed. The history stack visibly GROWS, newest snapshot on top.","sequence":{"actors":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:0:steps:2:sequence:actors","messages":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:0:steps:2:sequence:messages","shownCount":4,"activeIndex":3},"vars":[["text","\"AB\""],["stack ↑","[ mA, mAB ]"]]},{"view":"sequence","codeLine":4,"narration":"Now the User types \"C\", mutating the editor to \"ABC\", but does NOT save. The live text has moved past the last snapshot; the stack is unchanged.","sequence":{"actors":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:0:steps:2:sequence:actors","messages":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:0:steps:2:sequence:messages","shownCount":5,"activeIndex":4},"vars":[["text","\"ABC\" (unsaved)"],["stack","[ mA, mAB ]"]]},{"view":"sequence","codeLine":8,"narration":"The User hits undo(). The Editor asks History to pop(), the topmost memento (mAB) comes off the stack. The stack SHRINKS back to one.","sequence":{"actors":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:0:steps:2:sequence:actors","messages":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:0:steps:2:sequence:messages","shownCount":7,"activeIndex":6},"vars":[["stack ↓","[ mA ]"],["popped","mAB"]]},{"view":"sequence","codeLine":4,"narration":"History returns mAB and the Editor calls restore(mAB): text snaps back to \"AB\". Crucially, History handed back the token without ever reading what was inside it.","sequence":{"actors":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:0:steps:2:sequence:actors","messages":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:0:steps:2:sequence:messages","shownCount":8,"activeIndex":7},"vars":[["text","\"AB\" (restored)"],["stack","[ mA ]"]]},{"view":"class","codeLine":4,"narration":"Recap: the Editor created and read its own snapshots; the History grew and shrank a stack of opaque tokens it never opened. State crossed the boundary, knowledge did not, encapsulation intact.","classDiagram":{"classes":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:0:steps:0:classDiagram:classes","relations":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:0:steps:0:classDiagram:relations","shownClassIds":["orig","mem","care"],"shownRelIds":["creates","keeps"],"activeIds":["orig","mem","care"]},"vars":[["Originator","reads its snapshots"],["Caretaker","holds tokens it can't read ✓"]]}]}]}}]]}]
